--TEST--
`Core\Router::urlAbsolute`'s origin is the unit's `[[app]] origin` and nothing else in the file, let alone anything a request could carry — ADR 0102 § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0102 § 6 states the rule as a prohibition — an absolute link's origin is
// configured per mount and is *never* derived from `Host` or
// `X-Forwarded-Host` — and a prohibition is the hard half to assert: a program
// run off the command line receives no request, so there is no header here to
// fail to be read. What is assertable is the rule's positive half, and it is
// the same sentence: the origin has exactly one source, so a link's authority
// is decided before the program starts and no value the program computes, nor
// any other key in the file it computed it from, can move it.
//
// So this case surrounds the one key that counts with two that look like it.
// Every other near miss a file could once have held — an `origin` before any
// table, one under `[server]`, one under an `[app.dev]` sub-table — is now
// refused by the configuration reader itself before any of this runs (ADR 0064
// § 3's `deny_unknown_fields` over the typed tree), so what is left to get
// wrong is the two a *valid* file can still spell: a commented-out key, and a
// block keyed on one exact entry file that is not this program. The
// answer below has to be the third. A reader that took the first `origin` line
// it found, or any `origin` in any block whether or not it covers this entry
// file, passes the existing origin cases and fails here.
class Docs {
    // ADR 0096 § 1: the decision is a required sibling of every `#[Route]`, and
    // `Core\Audience::Public` is § 1a's case for a route that is genuinely open.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/docs/{page}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Docs::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(string $page, #[Core\Query] string $q = ""): string { return $page; }

    #[Core\Route(path: "/", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Docs::home")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function home(): string { return "home"; }
}

// The two spellings the file also holds. They are values in an array rather
// than two `if`s so the assertion below is a count over a set, which stays
// true when a third near miss is added to the file and the list beside it.
array<string> $decoys = [
    "https://commented-out.test",
    "https://other-entry.test",
];

array<string> $built = [
    Core\Router::urlAbsolute("Docs::show", ["page" => "intro"]),
    Core\Router::urlAbsolute("Docs::show", ["page" => "intro", "q" => "a b"]),
    Core\Router::urlAbsolute("Docs::home", []),
];

// Every link carries the configured origin, and not one carries either of the
// two the file also spells. The second count is what makes the first mean
// something: a reader that concatenated *every* `origin` it found would still
// satisfy the first.
var $rows = 0;
var $configured = 0;
var $leaked = 0;
foreach ($built as string $link) {
    $rows = $rows + 1;
    if (Core\Str::startsWith($link, "http://app-table.test:8080")) {
        $configured = $configured + 1;
    }
    foreach ($decoys as string $decoy) {
        if (Core\Str::contains($link, $decoy)) {
            $leaked = $leaked + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "configured=", $configured, "/", $rows, "\n";
echo "leaked=", $leaked, "\n";

// And the origin is carried across verbatim — scheme and explicit port
// included. `http` rather than `https`, and a port rather than the scheme's
// default, because § 6 makes the origin a configured *string* and not a host
// the runtime completes: a member that rebuilt the authority out of its parts
// would have a default to choose here and would choose wrong.
echo "link=", $built["0"], "\n";
echo "root=", $built["2"], "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# ADR 0102 § 6 gives an absolute link exactly one source, and ADR 0104 § 2 says
# which block it is: every block whose key covers the entry file, layered
# least-specific first, so the last one to state an `origin` wins. This file is
# two near misses around the one that answers.

# A block keyed on one exact file, § 1's narrowest key — and that file is this
# configuration itself rather than the program, so the block matches nothing
# here. It is the near miss a reader that took an `origin` out of any `[[app]]`
# block without asking which entry file the block covers would return, and § 2
# would layer it *last* if it did match.
[[app]]
entry = "nvs.toml"
origin = "https://other-entry.test"

[[app]]
root = "."
# Commented out, and a comment runs to the end of the line.
# origin = "https://commented-out.test"
origin = "http://app-table.test:8080"
--EXPECT--
configured=3/3
leaked=0
link=http://app-table.test:8080/docs/intro
root=http://app-table.test:8080/
